# rlmara.msg - Mara, the woman from the cellar (The Den); madam of the Gutter later
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# As madam of the Gutter: greeting and weekly report
{100}{}{Everyone in the house can leave whenever they want. Most of them don't. That's the only report that matters. The rest is numbers.}
{101}{}{Last week we had }
{102}{}{ customers. After costs: $}
{103}{}{. There's $}
{104}{}{ in the till.}

{105}{}{Ask. I keep the books honest.}
{106}{}{Let's go over how the house is run.}
{107}{}{Business outside these walls.}
{108}{}{Outside. All right. Keep your voice down.}
{109}{}{Something else.}
{110}{}{Give me the till.}
{111}{}{Let's talk prices.}
{112}{}{Let's talk about what our people get.}
{113}{}{How are our people?}
{114}{}{There's a problem?}
{115}{}{Later.}
{116}{}{Money?}
{117}{}{Let's talk about the house. What could we build?}
{118}{}{Let's talk about our people.}

# Payout
{120}{}{Here. $}
{121}{}{. Every coin of it earned by somebody who could have walked away.}
{122}{}{Nothing this week. We paid everyone first. That's how it works here.}
{123}{}{Go on.}
{124}{}{That's all.}

# Prices
{130}{}{The Den can't pay much. If we charge too little, we get the ones who think they bought a person. If we charge too much, the rooms stay empty.}
{131}{}{Cheap. Quantity over quality.}
{132}{}{The usual prices.}
{133}{}{Charge more. We're better than the rest.}
{134}{}{Leave it as it is.}
{135}{}{Right now we take whatever people can still scrape together.}
{136}{}{Right now we charge the usual.}
{137}{}{Right now we charge more than the others.}
{138}{}{Right now we charge prices nobody in the Den can pay.}
{139}{}{Starting next week.}

# Staff share
{140}{}{Right now our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. You know what I think about taking more.}
{142}{}{A quarter is enough.}
{143}{}{A third, like everyone else.}
{144}{}{Almost half. Whoever works well should live well.}
{145}{}{A quarter. (She says nothing for a long time.) Metzger took everything and called it protection. You take three quarters and call it business. I'll tell them. I won't tell them it's fair.}
{146}{}{A third. Like every other house in the Den. Nobody gets rich. Nobody starves.}
{147}{}{Almost half. (For the first time she smiles at you.) They'll remember this. So will I.}
{148}{}{Leave everything as it is.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They laugh. With each other, not just for the johns. I didn't know I'd missed that sound.}
{151}{}{They work, they complain, they sleep. That's more than most people in the Den get.}
{152}{}{They go quiet when I come in. I know that silence. I used to be part of it.}
{153}{}{One of them left last night. She had every right to. I just wish she'd had somewhere better to go.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is on Jet. She's stealing from the till already. I've seen where this ends. So have you.}
{161}{}{[Doctor] I'll look at her myself.}
{162}{}{Here's $200. Get her what she needs.}
{163}{}{Pay her in Jet instead of wages. At least she won't run.}
{164}{}{Throw her out.}
{165}{}{She screamed for three days. Now she's asleep. Nobody here will forget what you did for her.}
{166}{}{I'll take her to someone who knows about this. I can't promise she comes back.}
{167}{}{(Mara looks at you for a long moment.) No. Not in my house. (She takes the Jet off the table and throws it in the stove.) You want someone who does that, find another madam.}
{168}{}{She'll end up in the gutter. The real one. You know that.}

# Other crises
{170}{}{Something happened that I can't handle alone.}
{171}{}{Here's $300. Take care of it.}
{172}{}{I'll deal with it later.}
{173}{}{Money doesn't fix everything. It fixes this.}
{174}{}{I'll deal with Metzger's men myself.}
{175}{}{Send word to the Rangers. They owe us.}
{176}{}{You were waiting for them. Two of Metzger's men won't be coming back. The girls watched from the stairs. Nobody said a word, but they'll sleep tonight.}
{177}{}{The Rangers put two men on the roof for a week. I used to dream about Rangers on a roof. Now they're on mine.}
{181}{}{Soldiers in black armor. Drunk. They won't leave, and none of us can make them.}
{182}{}{Someone's hooked on Jet.}
{183}{}{They come and search the house. Not for the first time.}
{184}{}{A john beat one of ours. He says he'll be back.}
{185}{}{Something's going around. Two are already down.}
{186}{}{Money's missing from the till.}
{187}{}{Two are gone. Just gone.}
{188}{}{Metzger's men were here. They broke two doors and one arm, and they asked for me by name.}
{194}{}{[Debug] Show me the ending.}
{195}{}{[Debug] Add two empty rooms.}

# Look
{200}{}{A young woman with rope scars on her wrists and eyes that never stop moving.}
{201}{}{Mara. She got out of one of Metzger's caravans. She doesn't say how, and you don't ask.}

# Construction
{460}{}{What do we build? Start with doors that lock from the inside. Then we'll talk.}
{461}{}{The house itself and the furnishings.}
{462}{}{The business: security, staff, the books.}
{463}{}{Something you only get here in the Den.}
{464}{}{Which one?}
{465}{}{There's nothing to build right now.}
{466}{}{It costs $}
{467}{}{. There's $}
{468}{}{ in the till, and you're carrying $}
{469}{}{. Whatever the till can't cover comes out of your pocket.}
{470}{}{Build it.}
{471}{}{Not enough money for that. Back.}
{472}{}{We're already building: }
{473}{}{. Another }
{474}{}{ week(s).}
{475}{}{Good. It'll be done in }
{476}{}{ week(s). Until then there's hammering all day, and half the customers stay away.}
{477}{}{Back.}

# Staff and recruiter
{480}{}{We have }
{481}{}{ rooms and }
{482}{}{ people to work them.}
{483}{}{Nobody is out looking for new faces.}
{484}{}{Someone sits at Mom's and listens. The ones who want out come to us. Slowly. But they come.}
{485}{}{Our recruiter doesn't ask. I know what that means. So do you.}
{486}{}{Find someone who talks people into it. ($40 a week, $50 a head)}
{487}{}{Find someone who doesn't ask. ($40 a week, $50 a head)}
{488}{}{Let the recruiter go.}
{489}{}{Back.}
{490}{}{I know a woman at Mom's who hears every sad story in the Den. She'll send us the ones who want out. Nobody comes here in chains.}
{491}{}{(Mara's face goes still.) If that man brings in one person who didn't choose this, I'm gone. And I'm taking what I know to the Rangers.}
{492}{}{Done.}
{493}{}{Every room is taken. The recruiter is just costing us money.}
{494}{}{And there are }
{495}{}{ in the cellar. I can hear them at night.}
{496}{}{Send word to Metzger. There's room in the cellar. ($}
{497}{}{ a head)}
{498}{}{Done.}

# The route south
{600}{}{The route south.}
{601}{}{The Rangers hide somewhere in the NCR. My brother always said they'd come for us. We'll have to go to them.}
{602}{}{[Perception] I know where to look.}
{603}{}{We need a room nobody knows about before we can hide anyone. Build the hidden room.}
{604}{}{They're on the road. I won't sleep until we hear.}
{605}{}{There are people in the hidden room. So far we've gotten }
{606}{}{ group(s) through. Who takes them this time?}
{607}{}{I'll take them myself. [}
{608}{}{% chance]}
{609}{}{Back.}
{610}{}{An old garage in Downtown. Of course. Nobody looks at a ruin.}
{611}{}{They leave tonight. I'll walk them to the edge of the Den myself.}

# Chains, act 2: in the cellar
{800}{}{(She presses herself against the wall when you come closer.) You're the one who owns this place now. Essie says you're not like Metzger. Everybody says that about somebody.}
{801}{}{Who are you?}
{802}{}{Mara. I was in a caravan from the south. Twelve of us on one chain. When they stopped at the river, I got my hands loose. The others didn't. I still hear them when I sleep. (She looks at you.) What happens to me now is up to you. I can't go out there alone. Metzger's men know my face.}
{803}{}{The big one upstairs, Kolbe. He knows I'm here. He's waiting for your word.}
{811}{}{You can stay. Nobody will find you here.}
{812}{}{[Sneak] I'll get you out of the Den tonight. Nobody will see us.}
{813}{}{[Outdoorsman] I know a way south through the hills. You'll be in the NCR in a week.}
{814}{}{(Hand her over to Metzger.)}
{815}{}{I need to think.}
{816}{}{Stay.}
{817}{}{Uh.}
{820}{}{(She doesn't believe it. Then she does, a little.) Essie said you'd say that. She also said Metzger's men will come looking. Please don't let them find me.}
{821}{}{Nobody will.}
{830}{}{(For the first time she almost smiles.) The NCR. They say there are no chains there. I'll believe it when I see it. (You take her out through the back before dawn. Nobody follows. A week later she is gone into the Republic.)}
{831}{}{Good luck, Mara.}
{840}{}{(She understands before you say it. She doesn't scream. She looks at you until Metzger's men take her arms, and then she looks at the floor. In the morning there are five hundred dollars on the table.)}
{841}{}{...}

# Hidden in the Gutter
{850}{}{(Mara keeps her voice low.) I hear them upstairs, laughing. Some nights I almost forget where I am.}
{851}{}{Are you safe here?}
{852}{}{In the hidden room, yes. Essie brings me bread and news. Nobody has found the door.}
{853}{}{Tell me about the Rangers.}
{854}{}{There's a group in the NCR that hunts slavers. They hide in an old garage in Downtown. My brother used to say they'd come for us one day. They didn't. Maybe we go to them instead. There are others like me in the Den. There will be more.}
{855}{}{Stay hidden.}
{857}{}{Nobody's come down yet. Essie says they're asking questions all over the Den. The cellar won't hide me from someone who's really looking.}

# Back: madam of the Gutter (Phase 4, 3.5)
{860}{}{(Mara is standing in the doorway of the Gutter as if she's measuring it.) I came back. I told myself I wouldn't. The Den owes me nothing, and I owe it less.}
{861}{}{Why did you come back?}
{862}{}{Because every week another caravan comes through, and somebody has to stand at a door that opens from the inside. Essie is getting old. Somebody has to learn how she does it.}
{863}{}{But not while people work behind locked doors in your houses. Not in the Gutter, not anywhere. When that ends, ask me again.}
{864}{}{[Speech] Run the Gutter. With Essie, or after her. Your rules.}
{865}{}{You owe me this, Mara.}
{866}{}{My rules. Anyone can leave. Nobody gets locked in. Anyone who lays a hand on one of mine leaves through the back and doesn't come back. (She holds out her hand.) Then we have a deal.}
{867}{}{Stay. Please.}
{868}{}{(She shakes her head.) You don't know what you're asking yet. Come back when you do.}
{869}{}{Take your time.}
{870}{}{(Something closes in her face.) I owe you. Yes. That's how Metzger talked too. (She turns toward the door.) There's a woman in the NCR who fights people like you. Maybe I'll go and help her.}
{871}{}{...}

# The other towns (Umsetzung 6)
{620}{}{Where else could we open a house?}
{621}{}{Every town has people who need a door that opens from the inside. Which one?}
{622}{}{New Reno.}
{623}{}{Redding.}
{624}{}{Vault City.}
{625}{}{The NCR.}
{626}{}{San Francisco.}
{627}{}{Enough for now.}
{628}{}{Tell me about another town.}
{630}{}{New Reno. An old hotel on Virgin Street, the Silver Garter, across from the Cat's Paw. The families there own everything that breathes. Someone has to own it differently.}
{631}{}{Redding. The miners come up from the shafts with gold in their pockets and nothing in their eyes. There's an old tunnel behind the sheds in the mining camp.}
{632}{}{Vault City. A widow in the Courtyard, behind Cassidy's, has a cellar. The citizens who come at night are the same ones who make the laws in the morning.}
{633}{}{The NCR. Number nine in the Bazaar, next to the Rawhide. They have laws there. Laws can protect people. Sometimes.}
{634}{}{San Francisco. A sunk ferry terminal at the docks. The ones who wash up there have nowhere else left.}

